---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLocaleFromUrl } from '@/i18n/utils';
import { getBlogPost, blogChrome } from '@/i18n/blog';

const locale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url, Astro.currentLocale);
const post = getBlogPost('qr-code-printing-size-guide', locale);
const chrome = blogChrome[locale];
---

<Base title={post.title} description={post.description} schema="faq" faqItems={post.faqItems}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">{chrome.home}</a> › <a href="/blog/">{chrome.blog}</a> › <span>{post.breadcrumb}</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="blog-header">
        <p class="blog-meta">{post.meta}</p>
        <h1>{post.h1}</h1>
        <p class="blog-lede">{post.lede}</p>
      </header>

      {post.bodyNote && (
        <p class="blog-body-note">{post.bodyNote}</p>
      )}

      <div class="blog-content">
        <p>The single most common reason printed QR codes fail to scan is being printed too small. Not "the generator made a bad code" — the code is fine, the print shop just shrunk it below the scanner's working range. This guide gives you concrete minimum sizes for every common use case.</p>

        <h2>The 1:10 rule of QR code scanning</h2>
        <p>Rule of thumb: a QR code needs to be at least <strong>1/10th the distance from which it'll be scanned</strong>. Scanning distance of 100 cm (about arm's length) → minimum 10 cm wide. Scanning distance of 1 m → minimum 10 cm. Scanning distance of 5 m → minimum 50 cm.</p>
        <p>This is the floor. For reliable scanning — accounting for shaky hands, awkward angles, low-end phones — go 20-30% larger than the minimum.</p>

        <h2>Minimum sizes by use case</h2>
        <h3>Business card (scan distance ~15 cm)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>1.5 × 1.5 cm</strong> (about 0.6 × 0.6 inches). Recommended <strong>2 × 2 cm</strong>. Place on the back of the card or in a corner of the front, with a quiet zone of at least 4 modules of white space around it.</p>
        <h3>Restaurant table tent / menu (scan distance ~30 cm)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>3 × 3 cm</strong>. Recommended <strong>4 × 4 cm</strong>. Laminate for wipe-clean.</p>
        <h3>Product packaging (scan distance ~20-50 cm)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>2.5 × 2.5 cm</strong>. Recommended <strong>4 × 4 cm</strong> or larger. The bigger the better — packaging gets tossed, dropped, and viewed from awkward angles.</p>
        <h3>Poster / flyer (scan distance ~1-2 m)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>15 × 15 cm</strong> for 1 m scan distance. Recommended <strong>20 × 20 cm</strong> or larger. For a poster scanned at 3 m, go at least 40 × 40 cm.</p>
        <h3>Billboard / outdoor signage (scan distance 5+ m)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>50 × 50 cm</strong> for 5 m scan distance. For 10 m scan distance, minimum 1 m × 1 m. Outdoor codes should also use H (30%) error correction to handle UV, rain, and physical damage.</p>
        <h3>Conference badge / lanyard (scan distance ~30 cm)</h3>
        <p>Minimum <strong>3 × 3 cm</strong>. Recommended <strong>4 × 4 cm</strong>. The badge will be flipped and twisted — make it large enough to scan without precise alignment.</p>

        <h2>The "quiet zone" — the white border you must not crop</h2>
        <p>A QR code needs a clear white border on all four sides, called the "quiet zone." The minimum is <strong>4 modules wide</strong> (where one module is one black square in the code). Many designers crop this in the name of "clean design" — the code then fails to scan on some phones.</p>
        <p>When you place a QR code in a layout, give it 4-module padding on every side. Most generators (including ours) include the quiet zone in the exported image, so you don't need to add it yourself — just don't crop it off.</p>

        <h2>Resolution: what DPI to export</h2>
        <p>For print, export at <strong>300 DPI minimum</strong>. For large-format prints (posters, billboards), 150 DPI is acceptable if the source SVG is high-quality.</p>
        <p>When in doubt, export as <strong>SVG</strong> (vector). The print shop can scale it to any size without losing sharpness. Our generator supports SVG download.</p>

        <h2>Print materials: how to choose</h2>
        <p>Material choice affects durability, not scannability. The code scans the same whether it's on paper, vinyl, or aluminum — as long as the contrast is high.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Paper (business cards, flyers, menus):</strong> fine for indoor, short-term use. Glossier paper scans better than matte because the gloss keeps the white background clean.</li>
          <li><strong>Vinyl stickers:</strong> best for outdoor use. Laminated vinyl survives 1-3 years outdoors.</li>
          <li><strong>Aluminum / metal signs:</strong> for permanent outdoor or industrial installations. Most durable option.</li>
          <li><strong>Acrylic / plastic:</strong> for retail, restaurants, and reusable signage. Easy to clean.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid reflective materials (mirror-finish, glossy metallics) — they confuse the scanner. If you must use a reflective surface, request a matte laminate.</p>

        <h2>Color and contrast</h2>
        <p>The QR scanner reads <strong>contrast</strong>, not specific colors. The dark modules must be at least 70% darker than the light modules. Standard black on white is the safest. Other combinations that work:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Dark blue on white</li>
          <li>Black on yellow</li>
          <li>White on black (inverted — works, but some older scanners struggle)</li>
          <li>Dark green on light beige</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid: light-on-light, similar-value colors, gradients, low-contrast pairs (red on orange, blue on purple).</p>

        <h2>Test before you print 1,000</h2>
        <p>Print <strong>one</strong> copy first. Scan it with at least two different phones. Test in different lighting conditions (sun, shade, indoor, low light). Test at the angle users will actually scan from. If it scans reliably, then go to full print run.</p>
        <p>Cost of one test print: $0.50. Cost of reprinting 1,000 flyers: $300. The math is obvious.</p>

        <h2>{chrome.faqHeading}</h2>
        {post.faqItems.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.question}</summary>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}

        <p class="cta">Generate a print-ready QR code (SVG, 300 DPI, H error correction available). <a href="/#type=customize">Make a print-ready QR code →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .blog-post { padding: var(--space-lg) 0; }
  .breadcrumbs { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a { color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a:hover { color: var(--color-accent-strong); }
  .blog-header { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg); text-align: center; }
  .blog-meta { color: var(--color-fg-soft); font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .blog-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .blog-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); max-width: 640px; margin: var(--space-md) auto 0; }
  .blog-body-note {
    max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    color: var(--color-accent-strong);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .blog-content { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .blog-content h2 { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .blog-content h3 { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .blog-content p, .blog-content li { font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .blog-content details {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0; padding: var(--space-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--color-bg) 0%, var(--color-bg-soft) 100%);
  }
  .blog-content summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  .blog-content .cta { margin-top: var(--space-xl); padding: var(--space-md); background: var(--color-accent-soft); border-radius: var(--radius-md); text-align: center; }
  .blog-content .cta a { font-weight: 600; }
</style>
